WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.120
Saving every field is not enough if the
loader can combine fields from different

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:05.830
moments.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.720
New weights with old optimizer moments
and another cursor describe a state that

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.750
never existed.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.660
Overwriting one live file also risks
damaging the only copy when a write is

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.270
interrupted.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.910
The store therefore publishes
complete numbered generations,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.010
retaining an older valid generation.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.810
Each generation contains state,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.370
context and a receipt tying their
bytes and update index together.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.500
The recovery question is which complete
compatible state can be trusted now,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.670
rather than whether some
JSON happened to parse.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:49.362
The writer prepares a unique pending
directory next to the intended published

00:00:49.362 --> 00:00:50.332
directory.

00:00:50.762 --> 00:00:55.612
Each file is written, flushed and
synchronized before publication.

00:00:56.602 --> 00:00:58.772
It synchronizes the staging directory,

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:04.312
then renames that complete directory to
its numbered name on the same filesystem.

00:01:04.882 --> 00:01:08.572
The parent directory is
synchronized afterward on POSIX.

00:01:09.582 --> 00:01:11.992
The loader never chooses
a pending directory.

00:01:12.562 --> 00:01:14.952
Existing destinations are not overwritten,

00:01:15.342 --> 00:01:17.952
and the checkpoint store
assumes a single writer.

00:01:18.762 --> 00:01:23.922
These local primitives and fault tests do
not prove arbitrary storage or power loss

00:01:23.922 --> 00:01:24.592
behavior.

00:01:25.202 --> 00:01:30.462
A stale writer lock after a real process
failure needs inspection before saving

00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:31.052
again.

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:36.537
The demonstration first saves
update two hundred and sixty nine,

00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:40.817
then a second complete generation
at two hundred and seventy three.

00:01:41.707 --> 00:01:45.677
It saves update two hundred and
seventy four as generation three,

00:01:45.927 --> 00:01:49.157
then deliberately appends
corrupt bytes to its state file.

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:54.087
A separate pending directory contains
a truncated state with no completed

00:01:54.087 --> 00:01:54.897
publication.

00:01:55.607 --> 00:01:57.947
Those deliberate faults are
retained for inspection,

00:01:57.947 --> 00:02:01.717
rather than hidden by deleting
or replacing the bad files.

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:06.277
This is an injected damaged
and partial save scenario.

00:02:06.647 --> 00:02:11.567
It is not an observed machine crash or
proof of recovery after deleting a hosted

00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:12.097
runtime.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.423
The loader considers published
generations from newest to oldest.

00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:23.863
It reads the receipt and verifies member
hashes before accepting their state.

00:02:24.453 --> 00:02:29.243
Generation three fails because its
state bytes no longer match the receipt.

00:02:29.893 --> 00:02:31.403
The rejection reason is kept.

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:36.963
Generation two has matching bytes,
source and data context, schema,

00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:38.423
transform, configuration

00:02:39.173 --> 00:02:40.943
and consistent numerical state.

00:02:41.373 --> 00:02:42.903
It is accepted as a whole.

00:02:43.493 --> 00:02:47.973
The pending directory is listed as
incomplete rather than considered for

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:48.483
loading.

00:02:49.193 --> 00:02:53.593
The returned receipt names the loaded
generation and the rejected and ignored

00:02:53.593 --> 00:02:56.523
items, making fallback
visible to the caller.

00:02:58.813 --> 00:03:02.643
Recovery therefore resumes from
update two hundred and seventy three,

00:03:03.173 --> 00:03:04.363
cursor sixty four,

00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:07.863
rather than borrowing the
corrupted newer weights.

00:03:08.573 --> 00:03:12.623
The next batch and rate come
from that same older state,

00:03:12.953 --> 00:03:17.023
so update two hundred and seventy
four is recomputed consistently.

00:03:17.973 --> 00:03:22.646
The final numerical state still matches
the uninterrupted path at update eight

00:03:22.646 --> 00:03:23.363
hundred and thirteen.

00:03:24.153 --> 00:03:29.383
Rolling back one update loses a little
work but preserves the computation.

00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:35.913
Mixing a newer parameter file with an
older order or moment file would preserve

00:03:35.913 --> 00:03:36.503
neither.

00:03:37.353 --> 00:03:42.133
The receipt lets us distinguish genuine
rollback from an unexplained successful

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:43.203
looking restart.

00:03:45.522 --> 00:03:49.552
Change the expected training
hash or feature order instead.

00:03:50.142 --> 00:03:55.142
An older generation from another context
must not be treated as recovery of this

00:03:55.142 --> 00:03:55.872
experiment.

00:03:56.642 --> 00:04:01.802
The loader keeps looking for a compatible
generation and raises an explicit error if

00:04:01.802 --> 00:04:02.652
none remains.

00:04:03.182 --> 00:04:07.252
Unit checks exercise source,
data and schema mismatches,

00:04:07.422 --> 00:04:09.632
inconsistent cursor or schedule,

00:04:10.222 --> 00:04:12.812
failed state and failure
before publication.

00:04:13.462 --> 00:04:17.072
Passing those checks establishes
the implemented local behavior,

00:04:17.322 --> 00:04:21.592
while arbitrary device failure and
hosted recovery remain separate evidence.

00:04:22.502 --> 00:04:25.272
The useful habit is to
keep the loaded identity,

00:04:25.542 --> 00:04:30.982
fallback reason and scope visible instead
of compressing every restart into one

00:04:30.982 --> 00:04:32.052
success flag.

